<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <!-- DBT-P49-002: Name is "AetherCore", not "AetherCore Setup". The standard
       bootstrapper composes its window caption as "<Name> Setup", so a Name that
       already ended in "Setup" produced the observed "AetherCore Setup Setup".
       The bundle's own ARP display name is this Name, which should read as the
       product, not as an act of installing it. -->
  <Bundle
      Id="AetherCore.Setup"
      Name="AetherCore"
      Manufacturer="AetherCore"
      Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
      Compressed="yes"
      DisableModify="no"
      DisableRemove="no"
      Condition="VersionNT64 AND ((NTProductType = 1 AND WindowsBuildNumber &gt;= 22621) OR (NTProductType = 3 AND WindowsBuildNumber &gt;= 17763))">

    <!-- DBT-P49-003: without this the elevated child wrote its log into the
         unelevated user's %TEMP%, inheriting a DACL that gave that user
         FullControl over a file an administrator wrote (P71). A rooted Prefix
         moves the directory for the parent and the elevated child alike
         (Burn logging.cpp LoggingOpen, WiX v6.0.2).

         Why C:\Windows\Temp and not C:\ProgramData\AetherCore\logs:
         * [ProgramData] is not a Burn variable. It formats to "", which leaves
           \AetherCore\logs, a path relative to the drive root.
         * The unelevated parent opens its log first, so it would create
           C:\ProgramData\AetherCore and own it. The install hardener resets the
           DACL there but never the owner, and an owner can always rewrite the
           DACL.
         * Burn keeps its log open without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, which blocks the
           purge-data uninstall action from deleting that tree.
         The OS owns C:\Windows\Temp. Measured on the runner: each log here
         inherits only read access for Users, and a real non-admin was denied
         write, WRITE_DAC and WRITE_OWNER on it. That runner's image also grants
         Users:(F) on the directory itself. Whether a stock machine does is
         unmeasured, and DBT-P49-003 stays open until it is.

         [WindowsFolder] already ends in a backslash (Burn's ShelGetFolder). The measured
         DACLs are in docs/phase73/P73-REPORT.md. -->
    <Log Prefix="[WindowsFolder]Temp\AetherCore" Extension="log" />

    <!-- DBT-P49-002.

         LogoFile: the welcome screen showed WiX's 852-byte stock placeholder,
         a third mark after the shipped icon and the in-app mark of DBT-P48-001.
         It now carries the product's own AE mark, taken from the icon pipeline
         rather than authored a second time: apps/desktop/icons/64x64.png is
         generated by tools/icon-pipeline/build-icons.mjs and its sha256
         492020a6...ee28ca is the value recorded in apps/desktop/icons/SOURCE.json,
         verified equal on disk before this reference was added.

         LicenseUrl="": deliberately still empty, and this is NOT an oversight.
         The repository contains no PRODUCT license text. Cargo.toml declares
         "Apache-2.0 OR MIT" but no LICENSE or EULA file exists at the repository
         root or the workspace root. The only licence files in the tree are
         assets/models/licenses/{Apache-2.0.txt,Qwen-GGUF-NOTICE.txt}, which are
         the embedded Qwen model's licences, shipped as MSI payload because that
         model is redistributed inside the product. Showing either of those as
         the terms a user accepts before installing AetherCore would present an
         unrelated document as this product's licence.

         So there is nothing truthful to display, and an installer that shows
         invented licence text is worse than one that shows none. With
         hyperlinkLicense and an empty LicenseUrl the standard bootstrapper hides
         the link rather than rendering a dead one. This stays empty until the
         owner supplies the text; see docs/phase55/INSTALLER-UI.md. -->
    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
          Theme="hyperlinkLicense"
          LogoFile="$(var.LogoFile)"
          LicenseUrl=""
          ShowVersion="yes"
          SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <Variable Name="VcRuntimeVersion" Type="string" Value="" />
    <Variable Name="WebView2MachineVersion" Type="string" Value="" />
    <Variable Name="WebView2UserVersion" Type="string" Value="" />
    <!-- Value="" is required whenever Type is given (WIX0010). Without it this
         file does not compile at all, which is why AetherCoreSetup.exe had never
         been produced: the recorded x64 pipeline run (§46.16) is cargo -> tauri ->
         wix build -> wix msi validate -> payload check, and builds the MSI only.
         Proven pre-existing in §48.6 by building the pre-P48 file unchanged. -->
    <Variable Name="WindowsInstallationType" Type="string" Value="" />

    <!-- Microsoft documents pv > 0.0.0.0 in either location as the presence test. -->
    <util:RegistrySearch
        Id="WebView2MachineSearch"
        Variable="WebView2MachineVersion"
        Root="HKLM"
        Key="SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\Clients\{F3017226-FE2A-4295-8BDF-00C3A9A7E4C5}"
        Value="pv"
        Result="value" />
    <!-- DBT-P41-001: the VC++ runtime presence test, on the key Microsoft documents.
         Bitness="always64" reads the 64-bit view, which is where the x64 and arm64
         redistributables register. Runtimes\debug\* is the debug_nonredist tree and
         is deliberately not this key. -->
    <util:RegistrySearch
        Id="VcRuntimeSearch"
        Variable="VcRuntimeVersion"
        Root="HKLM"
        Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\VisualStudio\14.0\VC\Runtimes\$(sys.BUILDARCH)"
        Value="Version"
        Result="value"
        Bitness="always64" />
    <util:RegistrySearch
        Id="WindowsInstallationTypeSearch"
        Variable="WindowsInstallationType"
        Root="HKLM"
        Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion"
        Value="InstallationType"
        Result="value" />
    <util:RegistrySearch
        Id="WebView2UserSearch"
        Variable="WebView2UserVersion"
        Root="HKCU"
        Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\EdgeUpdate\Clients\{F3017226-FE2A-4295-8BDF-00C3A9A7E4C5}"
        Value="pv"
        Result="value" />

    <Chain>
      <!-- DBT-P41-001. Chained exactly as WebView2 is, and for the same reason: the
           product does not work without it and Microsoft's redistribution guidance is
           to run the redistributable as a prerequisite rather than copy the DLLs beside
           the application. App-local copies were considered and rejected in P47 §47.9 —
           Microsoft warns against that location because an app-local copy never
           receives a security update.

           Permanent="yes": other applications share these DLLs, so removing AetherCore
           must not remove a runtime they depend on. Same reasoning as WebView2.
           Vital="yes": if the runtime cannot be installed, the chain must stop rather
           than proceed to an MSI whose Launch condition will refuse anyway.
           /install /quiet /norestart are the redistributable's documented switches. -->
      <ExePackage
          Id="VCRedist"
          SourceFile="$(var.VcRedist)"
          DisplayName="Microsoft Visual C++ 2015-2022 Redistributable"
          InstallArguments="/install /quiet /norestart"
          PerMachine="yes"
          Permanent="yes"
          Vital="yes"
          DetectCondition="(NOT (VcRuntimeVersion = &quot;&quot;))" />
      <ExePackage
          Id="WebView2EvergreenBootstrapper"
          SourceFile="$(var.WebView2Bootstrapper)"
          DisplayName="Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime"
          InstallArguments="/silent /install"
          PerMachine="yes"
          Permanent="yes"
          Vital="yes"
          InstallCondition="NOT (WindowsInstallationType ~= &quot;Server Core&quot;)"
          DetectCondition="((NOT (WebView2MachineVersion = &quot;&quot;)) AND NOT (WebView2MachineVersion = &quot;0.0.0.0&quot;)) OR ((NOT (WebView2UserVersion = &quot;&quot;)) AND NOT (WebView2UserVersion = &quot;0.0.0.0&quot;))" />
      <!-- DBT-P49-002: progress sat on "Initializing..." with an empty bar for the
           whole 30-second install. The cause is here: the standard bootstrapper
           builds its progress line from the executing package's DisplayName, and
           this package had none, so the only package that actually executes on a
           machine where the two prerequisites are already present had nothing to
           report. The two ExePackages above always carried a DisplayName, which
           is why the uninstall path — where the MSI is named by its own
           ProductName — read correctly while the install path did not.

           Visible="no" is unchanged and unrelated: it keeps the MSI out of
           Add/Remove Programs so the bundle is the single entry a user removes. -->
      <MsiPackage
          Id="AetherCoreMsi"
          SourceFile="$(var.MsiPath)"
          DisplayName="AetherCore"
          Vital="yes"
          Visible="no" />
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
